PRIVATE REFLECTION: {The deliberation has shifted substantially. Main unresolved domain shift is GBIF training images (visible-light, color, varied illumination, likely sharp) vs deployment images (nighttime, fixed-brightness infrared, likely monochrome/grayscale, fixed camera setup). My previous belief favored C (tone-curve) for IR tonal remapping. After round 1, I now lean toward I (RGB channel swizzle) as the most important augmentation because IR removes reliable color information; RGB swizzling directly disrupts natural color-channel mappings and forces reliance on luminance, shape, and texture features that remain useful under IR. C only remaps luminance and does not remove color reliance, so it is a weaker runner-up. A/B linear brightness are less relevant because IR brightness is fixed.

The strongest competing answer is G (Gaussian blur). Colleagues 2,4,5,6,9 argue that the camera is focused at the midpoint of a 7 m ravine with 50 mm focal length and f/4, creating shallow depth of field, so animals at other distances will be out of focus; GBIF images are likely sharp. This became the majority position in round 1. However, the lens description is contradictory (“105mm wide angle lens with a 50mm focal length”), sensor size is unspecified, and the setup says animals will be “easily visible.” A uniform 11×11 Gaussian blur is also only a rough proxy for distance-dependent defocus. Exposure 1/500 s makes motion blur H negligible.

F (horizontal flip) remains a useful generic augmentation for left-right orientation at the pinch point, and votes were F=2, I=2, C=1, G=5 in round 1. But F does not address the primary visible-to-IR spectral gap. My current belief: I is most likely correct, with G as the strongest alternative and C/F as secondary. Key unresolved question: whether deployment IR images are truly grayscale (favors I) or merely have altered tonal relationships (favors C/G). Also unresolved: whether the optical setup actually produces shallow depth of field or deep depth of field due to wide-angle lens ambiguity.}

